            <ArticleTitle>Thymic Cavernous Hemangioma With a Left Innominate Vein Aneurysm.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>320-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2014.08.057</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(14)01773-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Here we report a case of thymic cavernous haemangioma with the left innominate vein aneurysm. A 43-year-old man presented with chest pain. Enhanced chest computed tomography revealed an anterior mediastinal tumor measuring 60 × 52 × 38 mm with multiple venous lakes and focal specks of calcification, composed of a low-density soft tissue mass along with a left innominate vein aneurysm. We preoperatively diagnosed the mass as a thymic hemangioma and subsequently performed surgical resection. Pathologic diagnosis was a thymic cavernous hemangioma with a left innominate vein aneurysm, which is very rare.</AbstractText>
